{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git log --oneline -1; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md'","aggregated_output":"3d32eb4 docs(space): fold the astra architecture review into the index observer note\n     1\t# dao.space.index as a dao.stream Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design note, 2026-09-13. `dao.space.index` is already the dao.stream\n     4\tobserver on the dao.space side: `snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` is one\n     5\tobserver run — attach at `:oldest`, fold to `blocked`, publish, keep nothing.\n     6\tThis note makes it a *stateful* observer, driven by\n     7\t`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` over *any* `dao.stream` medium. No\n     8\tnew namespace: everything here is index realization, which `dao.space.index`\n     9\talready owns. The library specified here is payload-agnostic: it sees d5 rows\n    10\tand a supplied ref schema, and nothing below knows or cares what the rows\n    11\tmean or who else reads the medium. Subordinate to\n    12\t[`dao.space.md`](./dao.space.md) (write path, three boundaries),\n    13\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) (§Composition), and the observer\n    14\tcoordination already specified by `dao.stream.observer`. It\n    15\tcomposes with [`yin.vm.macro.md`](./yin.vm.macro.md) §4.2, whose \"commit\n    16\t`program-in` first for durable provenance\" becomes an instance of this\n    17\tnote.\n    18\t\n    19\t**Why this note exists** — a composition it enables, not a fact the index\n    20\tknows: attach a `yin.vm` evaluator and `dao.space.index` to the same medium,\n    21\tand the VM constructs CESK state while the index materializes covered\n    22\tindexes over the same batches, so `dao.space.query/q` answers Datalog over\n    23\tthe program being executed, as it grows. Neither observer is aware of the\n    24\tother; the stream is the only coupling, and the index's ignorance of what it\n    25\tindexes is as load-bearing as the VM's ignorance of being indexed. That\n    26\tinvariant belongs to the composition and is stated where compositions are\n    27\t(`datom.world.md`, `dao.space.md` §Three Boundaries); this document\n    28\tspecifies only the index side.\n    29\t\n    30\t---\n    31\t\n    32\t## 0. Decisions\n    33\t\n    34\t1. **dao.space observes; it does not have to be the writer.** Today\n    35\t   indexing is \"the writer's duty\" over the writer's own local stream\n    36\t   (`dao.space.md` §The Write Path). That stays true *as a composition*, but\n    37\t   the mechanism becomes an observer over a medium — any medium, whoever\n    38\t   writes it and whoever else reads it.\n    39\t2. **One coordination loop, one library.** `dao.space.index` is driven by\n    40\t   `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` with its state in the `:consumer`\n    41\t   slot, exactly as an evaluator or the macro expander is. No second\n    42\t   observer machinery, and no new namespace: \"the dao.space observer\" is a\n    43\t   role `dao.space.index` plays when so driven, not a module.\n    44\t3. **The index is incremental and persistent.** Each observed batch is\n    45\t   folded into the four covered indexes as `dao.data.btree` values; the\n    46\t   trees are structurally shared across batches. `publish-index!`'s\n    47\t   full-rebuild-from-origin becomes a consequence of *starting* an observer\n    48\t   at `:oldest`, not the only way to build an index.\n    49\t4. **The observer never rewrites a datom and never allocates `t`.**\n    50\t   Transaction time is the writer's (the transactor's). The observer records\n    51\t   what it saw and *where* (its own batch ordinal) as facts *about* the\n    52\t   batch, in its own metadata namespace, never by editing rows.\n    53\t5. **Identity resolution is the observer's reading.** Tempids are\n    54\t   batch-local; the observer assigns durable ids per batch and records the\n    55\t   mapping as **resolution facts**. This is the one place cross-medium\n    56\t   identity can exist, because dao.space is the one observer that may read\n    57\t   every medium.\n    58\t6. **The index is payload-agnostic.** It interprets d5 shape and the\n    59\t   supplied ref schema, nothing else: it never evaluates, never inspects an\n    60\t   attribute's meaning, and never learns what another observer of the same\n    61\t   medium does with the rows. (This is what rules out the\n    62\t   \"CESK-in-the-index\" premise of `yin.vm-in-dao.space.md`: two interpreters\n    63\t   over one stream, not one interpreter with two faces.)\n    64\t\n    65\t---\n    66\t\n    67\t## 1. Today, and the gap\n    68\t\n    69\tAll of `dao.space` is on `dao.stream` (no v1 references remain). Its\n    70\tstream use has three shapes:\n    71\t\n    72\t| Piece | Shape today |\n    73\t|---|---|\n    74\t| `dao.space.transactor` | write-side interpreter over an agent's *own* local memory-log; wraps each transaction in one `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record; derives `t` by scanning the whole retained history at `create!` |\n    75\t| `dao.space.index/publish-index!` | `snapshot-datoms` the local stream **in full from `:oldest`**, build four btrees from scratch, append node blobs then manifest to an intake stream |\n    76\t| `dao.space.query/snapshot` | the read side's one v2 interpreter: mint `:oldest`, loop `observe/step` into a relation, stop at `blocked`/`ended`/`gap`/`defect`; a **one-shot** |\n    77\t\n    78\tThree consequences follow, and they are the gap:\n    79\t\n    80\t- **Nothing indexes a medium dao.space did not write.** Any other writer's\n    81\t  medium — a program stream, an expander's log — carries datoms nobody\n    82\t  indexes; querying them means a full `snapshot` each time, which is\n    83\t  Datalog *as of* a call, never over a medium as it grows.\n    84\t- **No index is incremental.** Every publication rebuilds from the origin;\n    85\t  every `create!` replays history for the watermark. The transactor's own\n    86\t  *Open items* names this as O(history) and asks for \"one truth — the log\n    87\t  carries its own checkpoint\". An observer that holds a persistent index\n    88\t  *is* that checkpoint's natural owner.\n    89\t- **`snapshot` is not a session.** It mints a fresh cursor per call and\n    90\t  cannot be resumed batch by batch, so it cannot participate in a\n    91\t  `run-on-stream` round the way an evaluator does.\n    92\t\n    93\t**Already true.** `dao.space.index` is the consumer side of observation.\n    94\t`snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` *is* an observer run: attach at\n    95\t`:oldest`, fold everything to `blocked`, publish once, discard the state —\n    96\tthe degenerate case of `run-on-stream` with a consumer that is always ready\n    97\tand keeps nothing between calls. `datoms-from-elements` already accepts both\n    98\telement shapes (raw d5 rows and transaction records), `index-datoms` already\n    99\tbuilds the in-memory index, and `observe/step` already exists.\n   100\t\n   101\t**Not yet true**, and it is code, not wording:\n   102\t\n   103\t1. *Incremental fold.* `index-datoms` builds a whole index from a whole datom\n   104\t   vector; `publish-index!` rebuilds from the origin every time. Being\n   105\t   driven batch by batch needs `fold-batch : index-state batch →\n   106\t   index-state'` over persistent btree values (§2.2).\n   107\t2. *Tempid resolution across batches.* A transactor-written medium carries\n   108\t   resolved ids; a medium written batch by batch without a transactor\n   109\t   carries per-batch negative tempids that *repeat*. `dao.space.index` never\n   110\t   meets that case today because it\n   111\t   only indexes the writer's own resolved log. Observing a medium it did not\n   112\t   write requires the per-batch allocator and resolution facts (§3) — a\n   113\t   correctness requirement, not a framing one.\n   114\t3. *Bad input as data.* `snapshot-datoms` throws on the first malformed\n   115\t   element, which is right for a one-shot over your own log and\n   116\t   head-of-line-blocks a session over someone else's medium (§2.2 step 1).\n   117\t\n   118\t---\n   119\t\n   120\t## 2. The index consumer (`dao.space.index`)\n   121\t\n   122\t### 2.1 The index state\n   123\t\n   124\t```\n   125\tindex-state = {:indexes  {:eavt bt :aevt bt :avet bt :vaet bt}   ; dao.data.btree values, structurally shared across batches\n   126\t               :storage  <IStorage over a content handle>       ; the trees' storage; persists across publishes (§4.1)\n   127\t               :mode     :resolved | :unresolved                 ; fixed at construction (§3.1)\n   128\t               :ids      nil | {:next-eid n}                     ; allocator; present only in :unresolved mode\n   129\t               :batch    n                                       ; ordinal of the next batch, observer-local\n   130\t               :schema   {attr {:db/valueType :db.type/ref ...}} ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n   131\t               :max-t    n                                       ; greatest writer t folded so far (§4.2)\n   132\t               :publish  nil | {:intake writer\n   133\t                                :staged nil | {:payloads [...] :next i}}   ; optional, §4.1\n   134\t               :defects  []}                                     ; rejected batches seen since last drain\n   135\t\n   136\t;; all in dao.space.index, beside index-datoms and publish-index!\n   137\tready?           (fn [x] (nil? (get-in x [:publish :staged])))\n   138\tload             index/fold-batch                              ; pure; §2.2\n   139\trun              index/flush-staged                            ; resumes a staged publication at :next; identity otherwise (§4.1)\n   140\tindex/publish!   index-state → index-state'                    ; explicit: stage this state's trees, then flush (§4.1)\n   141\tindex/drain      (fn [x] [(assoc x :defects []) (:defects x)])\n   142\tindex/db-value   index-state → query value                     ; §5: the covered trees as a dao.space.query source\n   143\tindex/checkpoint index-state → candidate                       ; §4.2: the value captured at a publication boundary\n   144\tindex/restore    candidate storage-opts → index-state          ; §4.2: resume from a verified checkpoint\n   145\t```\n   146\t\n   147\tDriven as `(run-on-stream {:observer o :consumer index-state} ready?\n   148\tindex/fold-batch index/flush-staged)` over any `dao.stream` reader\n   149\thandle attached through `dao.stream.observer/attach`. The coordination\n   150\tinspects no field of the index state; it drives it as readily as a VM —\n   151\twhich is the point of decision 2. **Publication is an explicit composition\n   152\tstep**, not a policy inside the loop: the composition calls `publish!`\n   153\tbetween rounds (after every *n* rounds, on `blocked`, on a timer — its\n   154\tchoice), and only the *resumption* of a staged publication lives inside\n   155\t`run`, so that `ready?` stays false until the intake has accepted every\n   156\tpayload. Calling `publish!` while a publication is still staged is a caller\n   157\terror (the composition checks `ready?` first); a staged publication is never\n   158\treplaced, only resumed (§4.1).\n   159\t\n   160\t### 2.2 `fold-batch`\n   161\t\n   162\tOne batch, one pure fold. **The fold is atomic per batch**: steps 0–2\n   163\tvalidate and plan against the *whole* normalized batch before step 3\n   164\ttouches a tree or step 4 advances `:ids`; a batch rejected at any step\n   165\tleaves all four trees and `:ids` unchanged, appends exactly one defect\n   166\t(batch ordinal, position, reason), and advances `:batch` by exactly one —\n   167\tnever zero, or every later cross-session ordinal (§3.2) would drift on the\n   168\tfirst malformed input even without a `gap`.\n   169\t\n   170\t0. **Normalize the outer value.** `run-on-stream` hands `load` one stream\n   171\t   value, and media differ in what that is: a transactor appends one\n   172\t   transaction-record map per `append!`; a program medium appends a vector\n   173\t   of rows. The grammar, checked in this order: a map containing\n   174\t   `:dao.space/transaction` is one element; a vector of exactly five whose\n   175\t   first slot is an integer and second a keyword is one d5 element (a bare\n   176\t   row is never mistaken for a five-row batch, and a five-row batch's first\n   177\t   slot is a vector, not an integer); any other sequential is a batch of\n   178\t   elements, each normalized by the same two rules and nothing else (no\n   179\t   nesting); anything else is a whole-batch defect at position `nil`.\n   180\t1. **Admit elements.** Each element is a d5 row or a\n   181\t   `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record, flattened. Admission is\n   182\t   *looser than* `datom/local-datom?`, which `element-datoms` applies today\n   183\t   and which requires a non-negative `e`: in `:unresolved` mode admission is\n   184\t   exactly `local-datom?` minus that one clause — integer `e` of either\n   185\t   sign, namespaced keyword `a`, `t ≥ 0`, integer `m`. `v` is unconstrained\n   186\t   by `local-datom?` already, so a negative ref-valued `v` needs no schema\n   187\t   knowledge to be *admitted*; the schema first matters at step 2. In\n   188\t   `:resolved` mode admission *is* `local-datom?`. Strictness is restored\n   189\t   after step 2: every row that reaches step 3 satisfies `local-datom?`. A\n   190\t   transaction-record element is admitted by the same per-datom rule applied\n   191\t   to each of its datoms plus the record's own shape checks (one shared `t`,\n   192\t   non-empty). Anything not admitted is a defect for the whole batch — the\n   193\t   session continues; a throwing `load` would leave the cursor on the bad\n   194\t   batch forever, the head-of-line hazard `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 11\n   195\t   removes. Bad input is data; a bug in the fold itself is the throw.\n   196\t   `datoms-from-elements` keeps its strict, throwing contract for the\n   197\t   one-shot path; the fold uses an admitting, non-throwing variant of the\n   198\t   same per-element rule.\n   199\t2. **Validate the mode and resolve identity** (§3). Identity lives in three\n   200\t   slots — `e`, a `v` under an attribute `:schema` declares a ref, and `m`,\n   201\t   the metadata *reference* — and the transactor already resolves all three\n   202\t   (`dao.space.transact` collects `m` tempids explicitly). The index does the\n   203\t   same; §3.1's matrix says what each slot may hold in each mode, and any\n   204\t   violation is a whole-batch defect. In `:unresolved` mode every tempid in\n   205\t   the batch — as `e`, as a declared-ref `v`, as `m`, or only as one of the\n   206\t   latter two (a reference to an entity the batch never describes) — is\n   207\t   mapped through a batch-local table to a fresh durable id from `:ids`, in\n   208\t   **first-occurrence order over the normalized batch** so allocation is\n   209\t   identical on every host, and the mapping is emitted as resolution facts.\n   210\t   Reserved ids (below `datom/first-user-id`, such as `default-op`) pass\n   211\t   through untouched in every slot. `:resolved` mode allocates nothing.\n   212\t3. **Fold.** Rows and resolution facts are `dao.data.btree/conj`ed into the\n   213\t   four trees — a persistent insert that shares every unmodified node with\n   214\t   the previous state. A node created by the insert carries no address mark;\n   215\t   dirtiness *is* that absence, a property of the node and of nothing else\n   216\t   (`btree.cljc` `node-store` skips every child slot that already carries an\n   217\t   address), so §4.1's `store-tree` later emits exactly what this and\n   218\t   subsequent folds created. Covered indexes are sets; a duplicate row is a\n   219\t   no-op (`conj` returns the same set). `:max-t` becomes the greater of\n   220\t   itself and the batch's greatest row `t`.\n   221\t4. **Advance** `:batch`, and `:ids` in `:unresolved` mode.\n   222\t\n   223\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\n   224\tbatch), on every host.\n   225\t\n   226\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   227\t\n   228\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\n   229\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\n   230\t\n   231\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\n   232\t|---|---|---|---|\n   233\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | the exact rows; `history` promises no ordering (its implementation preserves source order, EAVT over a tree), so order by `t` explicitly when it matters |\n   234\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\n   235\t\n   236\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\n   237\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\n   238\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\n   239\t\"what facts does this medium assert\" reads `current` and gets exactly that.\n   240\t`as-of` filters *both* observed rows and resolution facts numerically\n   241\tagainst `t`, though one carries writer time and the other the observer's\n   242\tordinal; a composition that uses `as-of` over a session's trees is\n   243\tfiltering two clocks with one number and should say which it means.\n   244\t**Retractions on such a medium**: an assertion and a later retraction of\n   245\tthe same `[e a v]` — the same *resolved* `e`; a reused negative tempid in a\n   246\tlater batch is a different entity and conflicts with nothing — both carry\n   247\t`t 0` and differ only in `m`, which\n   248\t`current-state-seq` rejects as conflicting history (`query.cljc`, the\n   249\t`m`-conflict throw). The index does not check for this — deciding whether\n   250\tan `m` value means \"retract\" is payload interpretation, decision 6 — so the\n   251\trows are folded and `history` answers correctly, while `current` over such\n   252\ta medium *throws* at query time. Loud, not silent; a composition that\n   253\tretracts on a medium without writer `t` has chosen the wrong medium for\n   254\t`current`.\n   255\t\n   256\t---\n   257\t\n   258\t## 3. Identity\n   259\t\n   260\t### 3.1 Why the observer must resolve\n   261\t\n   262\tA medium written batch by batch without a transactor carries negative\n   263\ttempids allocated *per batch* (from `(- datom/first-user-id)` downward, by\n   264\tconvention; the observer relies only on their being negative). Two\n   265\tconsecutive batches both contain `-16`. Folding them raw into one index\n   266\twould merge unrelated entities — the silent-wrong-data case.\n   267\t`dao.space.transact` resolves tempids within one transaction; the observer\n   268\tmust do the same per batch, and only the observer can, because only it\n   269\tholds the cross-batch allocator.\n   270\t\n   271\t**Why a mode, fixed at construction.** Durable ids are positive integers on\n   272\tone number line. A medium whose writer supplies positive ids — a\n   273\ttransactor's log, where ids are the caller's free choice (`transactor.cljc`\n   274\ttakes `:db/id` as given and derives a watermark over `t` only) — and an\n   275\tobserver minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index without\n   276\teventually colliding, and no watermark over `t` says anything about `e`. An\n   277\texplicit integer partition (writer even, observer odd, say) would avoid\n   278\truntime coordination, but only by changing the writer's allocation\n   279\tcontract, which an arbitrary existing writer has not agreed to; two modes\n   280\tare the simpler default and the one this note specifies. So an index state\n   281\tis either `:resolved` — it indexes media whose ids are already durable and\n   282\tallocates nothing — or `:unresolved` — it indexes media that carry only\n   283\ttempids and owns every positive user id in its index, allocating from\n   284\t`datom/first-user-id` upward.\n   285\t\n   286\t**The mode matrix.** Identity-bearing slots are `e`, a declared-ref `v`,\n   287\tand `m`. \"Reserved\" is any id below `datom/first-user-id` (`default-op`\n   288\tlives there); \"user-positive\" is any id at or above it; \"tempid\" is any\n   289\tnegative integer.\n   290\t\n   291\t| Slot | `:resolved` | `:unresolved` |\n   292\t|---|---|---|\n   293\t| `e` | user-positive; reserved or tempid → defect | tempid → allocated; reserved or user-positive → defect (an unexplained positive reference: the writer cannot know the observer's ids) |\n   294\t| declared-ref `v` | user-positive or reserved; tempid → defect (this is the schema-aware check `local-datom?` alone does not make) | tempid → allocated; reserved passes; user-positive → defect |\n   295\t| `m` | reserved or user-positive; tempid → defect | reserved passes; tempid → allocated; user-positive → defect |\n   296\t| undeclared `v` | any value, untouched | any value, untouched |\n   297\t\n   298\tA composition that needs both kinds of medium in one query opens two index\n   299\tstates as two sources; `dao.space.query` keeps sources as separate\n   300\tdb-values and joins only where the query says so. **Equating `?e` across\n   301\tindependently allocated sources is never meaningful** — not across a\n   302\t`:resolved` and an `:unresolved` source, not across two `:unresolved`\n   303\tsessions with separate allocators, not across two `:resolved` media with\n   304\tdifferent writers — because unification compares integers and carries no\n   305\tallocation-domain check. This is advisory in the query (nothing can enforce\n   306\tit there) and therefore a contract on compositions: joins across sources go\n   307\tthrough values. Phase 2's shared allocator is a *composition-owned value\n   308\tthreaded serially* through the `:unresolved` sessions that share it — never\n   309\tan atom the sessions share — and only such sessions may equate `?e`.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   312\t\n   313\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n* —\n   314\twhichever slot it appeared in — it asserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   315\t\n   316\t```\n   317\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   318\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   319\t```\n   320\t\n   321\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   322\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   323\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   324\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. That is a **contract on\n   325\tmedia, not a check in the index**: a medium must not assert attributes in\n   326\t`:dao.space.index/*`. The index does not inspect attribute namespaces\n   327\t(decision 6), so a medium that violates the reservation shares `[e a v]`\n   328\tkeys with resolution facts and owns the resulting `current` semantics\n   329\t(greatest-`t` shadowing, or the `m`-conflict throw on a same-`t` meet).\n   330\tWith the reservation honoured, resolution facts and observed rows have\n   331\tdisjoint `[e a v]` keys and can neither shadow nor conflict. These are\n   332\tfacts *about* observation, distinct from the observed rows. They make\n   333\t\"which batch asserted this entity\" — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]` — a\n   334\tquery, and they are the raw material for cross-medium provenance.\n   335\t\n   336\t**Refs are batch-local; cross-medium coordinates are values.** Refs are\n   337\tresolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   338\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   339\ttoday — and a declared ref *always* resolves within the batch it appears\n   340\tin. There is no way, at attribute granularity, to say \"this `-16` names an\n   341\tentity on another medium\": the index would allocate a batch-local entity\n   342\tfor it, and a shared allocator only prevents *collisions*, it establishes\n   343\tno *correspondence*. So a coordinate into another medium must be carried as\n   344\ta **value**, qualified by the batch it refers to, under an attribute the\n   345\tschema does not declare a ref; the join is then value-based over\n   346\tresolution facts — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   347\t:dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — and is the composition's, since only the\n   348\tcomposition knows which observer's ordinal `t'` corresponds to the\n   349\tproducer's batch number. That correspondence is a constant offset **only\n   350\twhile neither session has seen a `gap`**; a gap count records that gaps\n   351\toccurred, not how many batches were lost, so after one the offset must be\n   352\tre-derived or provenance across it marked unknown (open question 2).\n   353\t\n   354\tThis has a consequence for `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1, recorded here as a\n   355\t**required amendment** to that document: its `:yin/source-call` is written\n   356\tas both an external coordinate (initial expansion: an eid in the source\n   357\tbatch) and a log-local ref (nested expansion: a node in the log copy). One\n   358\tattribute cannot be both. It must split — an undeclared value attribute for\n   359\tthe external coordinate, qualified by `:yin/source-batch` as it already is,\n   360\tand a declared-ref attribute for the log-local node — so that\n   361\t`event-schema` declares only the latter a ref and the index resolves it\n   362\tbatch-locally while leaving the former as the value the join needs. The\n   363\tschema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   364\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, the amended `event-schema`); the\n   365\tindex learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An\n   366\tundeclared attribute holding a negative number is a value, not a ref, and is\n   367\tleft alone.\n   368\t\n   369\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   370\t\n   371\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   372\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   373\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   374\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   375\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   376\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   377\t\n   378\t---\n   379\t\n   380\t## 4. Publication and the checkpoint\n   381\t\n   382\t### 4.1 Incremental publication\n   383\t\n   384\tBecause `:indexes` are persistent `dao.data.btree` values, publishing after\n   385\tbatch *n* is `bt/store-tree` over each tree plus one manifest — the same\n   386\tnode-blob format and intake path `publish-index!` uses today. What makes it\n   387\t*incremental* is that the trees keep their `:storage` across publishes:\n   388\t`store-tree` stores only the **dirty subgraph** (its docstring: \"no-op\n   389\treturning the existing address when the set is already stored\"), so nodes\n   390\tunchanged since the last publication are never re-stored and never\n   391\tre-appended. `publish-index!` today rebuilds into a *fresh* recording\n   392\thandle each time, which is why every node is dirty and every publish is\n   393\tO(tree). Two things persist across publishes in the index state, and they\n   394\tmust not be confused: the **stored-address marks on the trees' nodes**,\n   395\twhich are what `store-tree` consults (dirtiness lives in the tree, not in\n   396\tthe storage), and the **identity of `:storage`**, so that a stored node is\n   397\tnever re-stored under a second handle. What does *not* persist is the\n   398\trecording handle's content: the blobs it recorded since the last flush are\n   399\texactly the payloads to append, in store order, and `flush-staged` drains\n   400\tthem once the intake has accepted the manifest, so the handle never grows\n   401\twith history. Append cost is therefore proportional to what changed;\n   402\t`jing/materialize!`'s dedup at the far end is a second line of defence, not\n   403\tthe mechanism.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Draining is safe only if nothing ever refaults through the drained\n   406\thandle.** `store-tree` needs nothing from it (marks live in the tree), but\n   407\t`-store-tree!` also overwrites the set wrapper's *restore source* with the\n   408\tstorage it was given (`btree.cljc`, `set! storage storage'`), and\n   409\t`resident-root` refaults an evicted root through that field. On the JVM a\n   410\troot restored from a durable store is held by a soft reference (the\n   411\t`kv-storage` default ref-type there); on a resumed checkpoint session the\n   412\tsequence *resume → publish through the recording handle → flush → drain →\n   413\tGC clears the root → next fold or query refaults the root through the\n   414\tdrained handle* ends in \"missing index segment\". Child slots are unaffected\n   415\t(they fault through the durable store the settings name). The invariant the\n   416\tindex state must keep: **a tree's refaults resolve against durable content,\n   417\tnever against the recording handle.** The mechanism is that every tree an\n   418\tindex session holds carries `Settings` with ref-type `:strong`, so a root is\n   419\tnever evicted and never refaults — and *where those settings come from\n   420\tdiffers by session kind*, which is the whole subtlety. Ref-pinning consults\n   421\tthe **tree's** settings, not the storage passed to `store-tree`, so a\n   422\t`:strong` recording handle pins nothing by itself:\n   423\t\n   424\t- a session started at `:oldest` builds its trees either in memory (settings\n   425\t  carry no storage; `make-store-ref` pins the raw node) or through its\n   426\t  recording `kv-storage` constructed with `:ref-type :strong` — safe either\n   427\t  way;\n   428\t- a **resumed** session must restore its trees through a\n   429\t  session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the durable content store\n   430\t  (`bt/restore-tree` directly, behind an index-side helper) — **not**\n   431\t  through `query/open-published!` / `restored-indexes`, whose storage takes\n   432\t  the host default, `:soft` on the JVM, because those are the *read* path\n   433\t  and a query consumer wants eviction. Restoring through the read path would\n   434\t  reproduce the sequence above exactly.\n   435\t\n   436\tRe-restoring the trees from the durable store after each flush was\n   437\tconsidered and rejected — \"append success means enqueued, not materialized\"\n   438\t(`dao.space.index.md`), so the durable store may not yet hold the blobs the\n   439\tnew manifest names, and `-restore` throws on absence. cljs and cljd default\n   440\tto `:strong` already and never see the hazard.\n   441\t\n   442\t**The staged publication is a state machine, not a list.** `publish!`\n   443\tstages `{:payloads [blob₁ … blobₖ manifest] :next 0}` — the blobs recorded\n   444\tsince the last flush in store order, the manifest last — and `flush-staged`\n   445\tappends from `:next`, advancing it after each `ok`, so a payload the intake\n   446\thas accepted is never appended again by an ordinary retry. `full` on payload\n   447\t*i* leaves `:next = i` and the state not-ready; the next round resumes at\n   448\t*i*. `closed`, `invalid-value`, a malformed outcome, and `transport-error`\n   449\tare outcomes this forwarder cannot continue from: it throws, with the\n   450\tstaged state — including `:next` — preserved in the thrown value, so the\n   451\tcomposition decides and a retry after a repaired intake resumes rather than\n   452\trepeats. Manifest-last still holds, so a partial prefix is never a\n   453\tpublication. The guarantee is **at-most-once acceptance per staged\n   454\toccurrence under ordinary retries**; content-address deduplication at\n   455\t`jing/materialize!` is a separate guarantee that makes an *abnormal*\n   456\tre-append (after a lost session, §7) harmless, not exactly-once. The\n   457\trecording handle's content is drained only when the manifest has been\n   458\taccepted, i.e. when `:next` reaches the end.\n   459\t\n   460\t### 4.2 The checkpoint\n   461\t\n   462\t`dao.space.transactor.md` *Open items* asks that \"the log carries its own\n   463\tcheckpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume\". A\n   464\tcheckpoint here is a *value with a validity contract*, in three parts.\n   465\t\n   466\t**Capture at one boundary.** `publish!` is called between rounds, when the\n   467\tsession's cursor sits exactly after the last batch its trees cover; it\n   468\treturns, with the staged publication, a **candidate**\n   469\t\n   470\t```\n   471\t{:manifest-address a  :cursor c  :ids i  :batch n  :max-t t\n   472\t :ingress-gaps g  :defects d  :mode m  :schema-hash h}\n   473\t```\n   474\t\n   475\tcaptured together in that moment: the manifest that will name the trees,\n   476\tthe cursor after the last folded batch, the allocator, the ordinal, the\n   477\tgreatest writer `t` folded (§2.2 step 3), the session's gap and defect\n   478\tcounts, and the mode and schema the trees were built under. Nothing may be\n   479\tfolded between the publish and the capture, and nothing is: capture *is*\n   480\tthe publish's return value. A composition that recorded a cursor later than\n   481\tthe manifest's coverage would, on restart, skip every batch in between\n   482\tpermanently.\n   483\t\n   484\t**A candidate becomes a checkpoint only when it is recoverable.** Intake\n   485\tsuccess means enqueued, not materialized (`dao.space.index.md`); a process\n   486\tcan fail with the manifest, or blobs it names, still in an in-memory intake\n   487\t— exactly the loss window `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance documents. A\n   488\tcandidate is promoted to a checkpoint by the composition, and only after it\n   489\thas verified against the durable content store that the manifest is present\n   490\tand every address reachable from it (`bt/walk-addresses`) resolves. Until\n   491\tthen the previous checkpoint stands. Recovery from a latest publication that\n   492\tnever became recoverable is therefore never a special case: the composition\n   493\tresumes from the last *verified* checkpoint and re-folds the suffix.\n   494\t\n   495\t**Resumption is bound.** `restore` refuses a checkpoint whose `:mode` or\n   496\t`:schema-hash` differ from the session being constructed, restores the four\n   497\ttrees lazily through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the\n   498\tdurable store (§4.1 — not the query read path), reinstates `:ids`,\n   499\t`:batch`, `:max-t`, `:ingress-gaps` and `:defects` (a partial index stays\n   500\tpartial after restart; a fresh zero would make §3.2's offset check look safe\n   501\tagain), re-attaches at `c`, and continues folding. Nothing is replayed from\n   502\tthe origin.\n   503\t\n   504\t**The watermark.** The transactor's `create!` needs \"one plus the greatest\n   505\t`t` in the retained history\". A checkpoint covering `t ≤ 10` cannot answer\n   506\tthat alone if transactions through `t = 12` exist after its cursor. So the\n   507\tanswer is `:max-t` — a writer-time summary derived from the folded rows at\n   508\tcapture, causally bound to `:cursor`, never derived from observer ordinals\n   509\t— *plus the fold of the retained suffix after `c`* before writes are\n   510\tenabled. Three cases, distinct:\n   511\t\n   512\t- **Same logical stream, reopened in-process** (the writer-task restart\n   513\t  `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance names): restore the checkpoint, fold the\n   514\t  suffix from `c` to `blocked`, then `max-t + 1` is the watermark. This is\n   515\t  the O(suffix) relief the transactor asked for.\n   516\t- **Process restart over a durable medium** (`dao.stream.file.md`, cursors\n   517\t  survive): the same, over the reopened medium.\n   518\t- **Process restart over a memory-log**: a new, empty logical stream with a\n   519\t  new identity; the checkpoint's cursor names nothing on it, the session\n   520\t  starts at `:oldest`, and the watermark is the transactor's today (`0` for\n   521\t  empty history).\n   522\t\n   523\t\"One truth\" is honoured rather than assumed: `:max-t` is not a second\n   524\tcounter that can disagree with the log; it is the log's own `t`, summarized\n   525\tat a named cursor and only ever advanced by folding the log. Where the\n   526\tcheckpoint *record* lives — on the observed medium or beside the manifest\n   527\tin `dao.jing` — remains open (§8); its validity conditions above do not.\n   528\t\n   529\t---\n   530\t\n   531\t## 5. Relationship to the transactor and to `publish-index!`\n   532\t\n   533\tThe transactor remains the writer and the owner of `t`. \"Indexing is the\n   534\twriter's duty\" becomes: *the writer runs `dao.space.index` as an observer\n   535\tover its own stream* — one index state attached to its local memory-log,\n   536\tpublishing on its policy. `publish-index!` is then a convenience over the\n   537\tsame functions: attach at `:oldest`, `fold-batch` to `blocked`, publish\n   538\tonce, discard the state. Its full-history behaviour is preserved as the\n   539\tdegenerate case of an observer that never kept state, and its manifest\n   540\tformat, intake path, and retry-safety are unchanged.\n   541\t\n   542\t`dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`,\n   543\t`db-value`, `checkpoint`, `restore`, the admitting per-element rule, and the\n   544\tresolution-fact attributes; it loses nothing. Nothing about `dao.jing`\n   545\tchanges: it still materializes opaque payloads from intake pools and never\n   546\tinterprets.\n   547\t\n   548\t**`dao.space.query` gains one source kind.** An index state is not a query\n   549\tvalue today: `query/value?` accepts relations, views, and *opened\n   550\tpublished* values, and `realize-db-value!` rejects anything else — carrying\n   551\t`:indexes` does not make a map a source. Wrapping the EAVT rows in\n   552\t`query/relation` would work but would materialize every row and bypass the\n   553\tcovered trees. So `index/db-value` produces an **in-process covered value**:\n   554\tthe same shape `open-published!` builds (four restored trees plus rows\n   555\tdeferred behind a delay), constructed over the session's live trees instead\n   556\tof a manifest, and `query` recognises it through the same covered-index\n   557\trealization path. Dependency direction is preserved — `query` already\n   558\trequires `index`; `index` builds a value `query` accepts, and the value's\n   559\ttrees are persistent, so a `db-value` taken before a fold is unchanged by\n   560\tit.\n   561\t\n   562\t**What \"live\" means, precisely.** After a successful `run-on-stream` round,\n   563\t`(index/db-value state)` represents every batch the session has admitted\n   564\tand folded up to its cursor — nothing more. It excludes whole rejected\n   565\tbatches (`:defects` says which) and reports gaps (`:ingress-gaps`). It\n   566\tpromises nothing about appends the observer has not yet read, and nothing\n   567\tabout a sibling evaluator's progress over the same medium: \"the index is\n   568\tcurrent with the medium\" is a statement about the observer's cursor, and a\n   569\ttest that wants to see a batch must *drive the observer* first, then query. The three boundaries of `dao.space.md` are untouched;\n   570\twhat moves is *when* the transactor-side index is built (continuously,\n   571\tnot at publish) and *over what* (any medium, not only the writer's own).\n   572\t\n   573\t---\n   574\t\n   575\t## 6. Invariants\n   576\t\n   577\t| Invariant | How the observer honours it |\n   578\t|---|---|\n   579\t| No hidden global state | Allocator, batch ordinal, trees, staged publication — all fields of one index-state value threaded through `run-on-stream`. No registry of observers; a composition holds the states it created. |\n   580\t| No implicit control flow | Indexing happens in exactly one place: `fold-batch` inside the observe step. Nothing indexes on write, on query, or on publish. |\n   581\t| No callbacks | `fold-batch` is a function (index-state, batch) → index-state'. The driver is `run-on-stream`; cadence is the composition's. |\n   582\t| No shared mutable state | `dao.data.btree` values are persistent; two sessions never share a tree by reference they could both mutate. The recording handle's identity persists with the session and its content is drained at each accepted manifest (§4.1); a shared allocator is a value threaded serially, never an atom (§3.1). |\n   583\t| No layer collapsing | *append* (writer) → medium → *observe/fold* (this note) → *publish* (intake) → *materialize* (`dao.jing`) → *query*. The VM is a sibling observer, not a stage. |\n   584\t| No assumed graphs | Ref resolution uses only the supplied `:schema`; the observer does not infer which values are refs. Batch shape is validated by the admitting per-element rule and the outer grammar of §2.2 step 0; malformed input is a defect, not a guess. |\n   585\t\n   586\t---\n   587\t\n   588\t## 7. Phases\n   589\t\n   590\t**Phase 0 — relocate the coordination loop (done 2026-09-13).** The\n   591\t`attach`/`observe-next`/`run-on-stream` loop lived at\n   592\t`yin.vm.stream-observer` because the VM was its first consumer, but its\n   593\town docstring says it inspects no evaluator field, and a `dao.space`\n   594\tnamespace requiring `yin.vm.*` would invert the layering (`dao.stream` →\n   595\t`dao.space` → `yin.vm`, never back). It is now `dao.stream.observer`\n   596\t(`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc`, test\n   597\t`test/dao/stream/observer_test.cljc`), beside `dao.stream.observe`\n   598\twhose single `step` it loops over. The `yin.vm` evaluators, the REPL, and\n   599\tthe macro expander require it from there; `yin.vm` keeps no observer code\n   600\tof its own. The pending fix from `yin.vm.macro.md` §5 — keep the throw,\n   601\tcarry the partial `{:observer :consumer}` session in `ex-data` — lands in this\n   602\tnamespace.\n   603\t\n   604\t**Phase 0′ — parity over the transactor's medium.** *Gated on the\n   605\t`run-on-stream` partial-session fix landing first* (Phase 0's pending item):\n   606\ta round that finishes a staged publication and then throws on a later read\n   607\tor fold would otherwise hand the caller its pre-round session, and a retry\n   608\twould repeat the accepted publication. `dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`,\n   609\t`publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, `db-value`, `checkpoint`, `restore`,\n   610\tthe admitting element rule and outer grammar, and the resolution facts; a\n   611\t`:resolved` session attached at `:oldest` over a transactor's local\n   612\tmemory-log produces trees **logically equal** to `index-datoms` over\n   613\t`snapshot-datoms` of the same log — the same rows, counts, and query results\n   614\tover `db-value`, and a valid restore of what it publishes. *Not* an\n   615\tidentical manifest: `publish-index!` bulk-builds with `from-sequential`\n   616\twhile the session inserts with `conj`, and the two partition the same rows\n   617\tdifferently (a JVM probe at branching factor 4 confirms equal rows, unequal\n   618\troot blobs); canonical layout would be a new design requirement, not a\n   619\tproperty of the tree. Tests must include enough rows and insertion orders\n   620\tto force splits. Further tests: outer grammar — a bare record, a bare d5\n   621\trow, a vector of both, a malformed outer value, a malformed nested record,\n   622\ta valid prefix followed by a defect (trees and `:ids` unchanged, one defect,\n   623\t`:batch` advanced once), valid–invalid–valid batches; the mode matrix, one\n   624\trow per cell, on both modes; `m` tempids resolved and joined; deterministic\n   625\tallocation order across hosts; `run-on-stream` `blocked`/`end` behaviour;\n   626\tthe publication state machine — `ok/full/full/ok`, refusal at the manifest,\n   627\ta terminal outcome after accepted blobs with `:next` preserved in the\n   628\tthrow, a successful resumption followed by a read failure, and a second\n   629\t`publish!` after more batches appending only the blobs stored since the\n   630\tfirst (count them); the **checkpoint candidate** round-trips through\n   631\t`pr-str`/`read-string` (it, unlike a live session, is plain data), a\n   632\tcandidate whose manifest is not yet materialized is *not* promoted, and\n   633\t`restore` refuses a mismatched mode or schema; the F2 hazard pinned\n   634\tdeterministically both ways through the btree's `:test` ref seam (`:test`\n   635\tref-type plus `clear-test-refs!`): a resumed session restored through a\n   636\t`:test`-ref storage, published, drained, refs cleared → the next query\n   637\t*throws* \"missing index segment\"; the same sequence through the\n   638\tsession-constructed `:strong` storage → no throw; and, gated to the JVM\n   639\twhere the read path is not already `:strong`, a session restored through\n   640\t`restored-indexes` carries `bt/default-ref-type*`.\n   641\t\n   642\t**Phase 1 — a medium with batch-local tempids (composition test).** The\n   643\tindex is exercised over a medium another observer also reads: two\n   644\t`dao.stream.observer` sessions on one `program-out`, one driving\n   645\t`ast-walker`, one driving `dao.space.index` in `:unresolved` mode. The\n   646\tindex code under test knows nothing of the VM; the test does. Tests:\n   647\tnegative `e` rows are admitted and resolved, positive `e` on this medium is\n   648\ta defect; the VM's value and the\n   649\tindex's `current` view from the same batches; after each observer round —\n   650\tthe test drives the indexer, then queries; appending drives nothing — `q`\n   651\tover `db-value` sees every batch folded up to the cursor and a `db-value`\n   652\ttaken before the round is unchanged; two batches with overlapping\n   653\ttempids index as distinct entities with correct resolution facts;\n   654\t`[?e :yin/type :lambda]` finds every lambda the program contained; a ref\n   655\tattribute resolved, an undeclared negative value untouched; `history`\n   656\tdocumented as degenerate over `t 0` rows.\n   657\t\n   658\t**Phase 2 — cross-medium provenance.** Prerequisite: the `yin.vm.macro.md`\n   659\t§4.1 amendment (§3.2) splitting `:yin/source-call` into a value attribute\n   660\tfor external coordinates and a ref attribute for log-local nodes. Observers\n   661\tover `program-in`, `program-out`, and the macro log, the `:unresolved`\n   662\tsessions sharing one composition-owned allocator threaded serially; the\n   663\toffset fact between the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal. Tests:\n   664\tan initial expansion's external coordinate joins to the source entity\n   665\tthrough resolution facts by value; a nested expansion's log-local ref\n   666\tresolves within the log batch; the same negative number on two media names\n   667\ttwo entities; a `v`-only and an `m`-only tempid each get an id and facts;\n   668\tthe `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 chain source-call → event → expansion-root\n   669\tanswered by `dao.space.query/q` over the three indexed media; after a\n   670\tforced `gap` on one session the offset is reported void.\n   671\t\n   672\t**Phase 3 — checkpoint validity and recovery.** Over a file-backed medium\n   673\t(`dao.stream.file`): capture a candidate, verify it against the durable\n   674\tstore, restart from it, fold the suffix, and assert the resumed index\n   675\tequals a from-origin index and the watermark equals `max-t + 1` over\n   676\tcheckpoint plus suffix; a candidate whose manifest was never materialized is\n   677\tnot promoted and restart uses the previous checkpoint; restored\n   678\t`:ingress-gaps`/`:defects` survive; a same-logical-stream in-process reopen\n   679\trestores, folds the suffix, and only then enables writes. Over a\n   680\tmemory-log, assert the documented behaviour instead: a resumed session\n   681\tstarts fresh at `:oldest` of the new log and the checkpoint is not\n   682\tconsulted. The transactor's `create!` derives its watermark from a\n   683\tcheckpoint plus suffix when one is offered.\n   684\t\n   685\t---\n   686\t\n   687\t## 8. Open questions\n   688\t\n   689\t- **One session per medium, or one over a merged view?** Per medium keeps\n   690\t  each cursor honest and makes `gap` attributable; a merged view needs a\n   691\t  merge interpreter that `dao.stream.md` does not (and should not) provide.\n   692\t  This note leans per-medium with a shared allocator, but the allocator\n   693\t  then couples the sessions — a composition choice to state when it is\n   694\t  made.\n   695\t- **Retention and `gap`.** Over an evicting transport the observer inherits\n   696\t  `dao.stream.observer`'s gap accounting; an index with a gap is a partial\n   697\t  index and must say so, and a cross-session ordinal offset (§3.2) is void\n   698\t  after one. Whether a published manifest should carry the gap count is\n   699\t  undecided.\n   700\t- **Where the checkpoint lives** (§4.2).\n   701\t- **Whether `snapshot` is retired** in favour of \"run a throwaway session to\n   702\t  `blocked` and take its trees\". Probably, but `snapshot`'s status\n   703\t  vocabulary (`:ended`/`:blocked`/`:gap`/`:defect`) should survive as the\n   704\t  session's terminal report.\n   705\t\n   706\t---\n   707\t\n   708\t## Appendix A. Review findings and their resolution\n   709\t\n   710\tRound 1 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `5296ee5`; glm-5.3 verified every mechanism claim against the code (all nine hold, claim 9 partially) and requested changes on:\n   711\t\n   712\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   713\t|---|---|---|\n   714\t| F1 | Phase 0′'s `pr-str`/`read-string` round trip of a live session cannot pass: `BTSet` prints elements-only, a storage handle is unprintable (glm P2) | Phase 0′ round-trips the *checkpoint value* instead and compares `restored-indexes` to the live trees |\n   715\t| F2 | Draining the recording handle is unsafe on the resumed-checkpoint path on the JVM: `-store-tree!` rewrites the wrapper's restore source and a soft-ref'd root refaults through the drained handle (glm P2) | §4.1 states the invariant (refaults resolve against durable content, never the handle) and pins index-session roots `:strong`; re-restore after flush rejected because materialization is asynchronous; Phase 0′ test |\n   716\t| F3 | The `:dao.space.index/*` reservation is a convention the index cannot check (glm P3) | §3.2 states it as a contract on media, with the consequence of violating it |\n   717\t| F4 | Assert-then-retract on a `t 0` medium hits `current`'s `m`-conflict throw (glm P3) | §2.3 states it: folded, `history` correct, `current` throws loudly; not a defect, since recognising a retraction is payload interpretation |\n   718\t| F5 | \"A query, not a scan\" overstates; \"dirty-tracked against storage\" misplaces dirtiness; tx records and `v`-only tempids on `:unresolved` media inferred, not stated (glm P3) | §4.2 and §2.2 reworded; both cases stated in §2.2 steps 1–2 |\n   719\t\n   720\tRound 2 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `280dda2`; F1, F3, F4, F5 confirmed resolved, F2 partially:\n   721\t\n   722\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   723\t|---|---|---|\n   724\t| N1 | The `:strong` remedy binds to the session's recording storage, but ref-pinning consults the *tree's* settings; a session resumed via `open-published!`/`restored-indexes` gets the JVM `:soft` default and the F2 sequence survives (glm P2) | §4.1 states where settings come from per session kind; a resumed session restores through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage`, never the query read path; §4.2 reworded |\n   725\t| N2 | \"Survives a forced GC\" is not a deterministic test; soft clearing cannot be forced (glm P3) | Phase 0′ pins the hazard both ways through the `:test` ref-type and `clear-test-refs!` |\n   726\t\n   727\tRound 3 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer-r3.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `0bd7550` — **APPROVE** (glm-5.3); N1, N2 confirmed resolved and the resumed path verified closed against `btree.cljc` with no other refault route:\n   728\t\n   729\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   730\t|---|---|---|\n   731\t| N3 | The \"wrong construction\" assertion is vacuous off the JVM, where the read path already defaults `:strong` (glm P3) | Phase 0′ asserts against `bt/default-ref-type*` and gates that assertion to the JVM |\n   732\t\n   733\tArchitecture round (`collab/1789289314611-architect-review-index-as-observer-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`, thread `01a099f1-87d6-7811-8a69-b3336b956fac`, resumed after a provider usage-limit failure), on `e101932`; gpt-6-astra confirmed symmetric ignorance, the core boundaries, the dirty-subgraph claim, the refault fix, and the checkpoint/serialization corrections, and requested changes on:\n   734\t\n   735\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   736\t|---|---|---|\n   737\t| A1 | Resolution omits the `m` slot; a macro log's `m = ev` (a negative event id) would stay unresolved and disconnect provenance; `local-datom?` cannot catch it (astra P1) | §2.2 step 2 resolves all three identity slots including `m`-only tempids; §3.1 matrix has an `m` row; Phase 0′/2 tests |\n   738\t| A2 | `:yin/source-call` declared a ref resolves log-locally; a shared allocator prevents collisions, not correspondence; one attribute cannot be both an external coordinate and a log-local ref (astra P1) | §3.2: refs are batch-local, cross-medium coordinates are values qualified by batch, the join is the composition's; a **required amendment** to `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 splitting the attribute is recorded; Phase 2 gated on it |\n   739\t| A3 | The checkpoint had no validity contract: enqueued ≠ recoverable; four fields not captured at one boundary; incompleteness not preserved (astra P1) | §4.2 rewritten: candidate captured as `publish!`'s return value, promoted only after verifying the manifest and every reachable blob in the durable store, resumption bound to mode/schema and restoring gap/defect counts; Phase 3 tests |\n   740\t| A4 | \"Retain the exact list\" is not per-payload progress; the `run-on-stream` progress defect bites an indexer whose round finishes a publication then throws (astra P2) | §4.1 state machine `{:payloads :next}`, advance per `ok`, terminal outcomes throw with `:next` preserved; at-most-once per staged occurrence stated as the guarantee; Phase 0′ gated on the observer fix |\n   741\t| A5 | The index state is not a `query/value?`; \"after each appended batch\" is not an observation boundary (astra P2) | §5: `index/db-value` builds an in-process covered value on the `open-published!` shape; \"live\" defined as every batch folded up to the cursor after a round; Phase 1 test reworded |\n   742\t| A6 | The mode rule missed positive declared-ref `v` on `:unresolved` media, tempid `v` on `:resolved` media, id zero, and `m` (astra P2) | §3.1 mode matrix over `e`, declared-ref `v`, `m`, with reserved ids |\n   743\t| A7 | A checkpoint alone cannot yield the writer watermark when retained transactions follow its cursor; the in-process task restart was excluded (astra P2) | §4.2: `:max-t` at capture plus the folded suffix; three restart cases distinguished; \"one truth\" argued, not assumed |\n   744\t| A8 | Outer batch grammar unspecified (a record map iterated as a batch would see map entries); rejected batch's state transition unspecified (astra P2) | §2.2 step 0 grammar; atomic-per-batch rule with `:batch` advanced exactly once |\n   745\t| A9 | Manifest equality is not a valid acceptance test: `from-sequential` and `conj` partition the same rows differently (JVM probe) (astra P2) | Phase 0′ requires logical equality — rows, counts, query results, valid restore — with enough rows to force splits |\n   746\t| A10 | `history` promises no `t` order; `as-of` mixes two clocks; the retraction example must be qualified by resolved identity (astra P3) | §2.3 reworded |\n   747\t| A11 | Even/odd partition is a real alternative; \"never equate `?e`\" is advisory for *any* independent sources; two invariant-table entries stale; shared allocator must be a threaded value (astra P3) | §3.1 and §6 reworded |\n   748\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '271,529p'; rg -n 'defn walk-addresses|durab|sync|fsync|materialize' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc docs/design/dao.jing.md | head -50; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc | sed -n '2101,2120p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '271,529p'; rg -n 'defn walk-addresses|durab|sync|fsync|materialize' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc docs/design/dao.jing.md | head -50; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc | sed -n '2101,2120p'\"","aggregated_output":"   271\t**Why a mode, fixed at construction.** Durable ids are positive integers on\n   272\tone number line. A medium whose writer supplies positive ids — a\n   273\ttransactor's log, where ids are the caller's free choice (`transactor.cljc`\n   274\ttakes `:db/id` as given and derives a watermark over `t` only) — and an\n   275\tobserver minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index without\n   276\teventually colliding, and no watermark over `t` says anything about `e`. An\n   277\texplicit integer partition (writer even, observer odd, say) would avoid\n   278\truntime coordination, but only by changing the writer's allocation\n   279\tcontract, which an arbitrary existing writer has not agreed to; two modes\n   280\tare the simpler default and the one this note specifies. So an index state\n   281\tis either `:resolved` — it indexes media whose ids are already durable and\n   282\tallocates nothing — or `:unresolved` — it indexes media that carry only\n   283\ttempids and owns every positive user id in its index, allocating from\n   284\t`datom/first-user-id` upward.\n   285\t\n   286\t**The mode matrix.** Identity-bearing slots are `e`, a declared-ref `v`,\n   287\tand `m`. \"Reserved\" is any id below `datom/first-user-id` (`default-op`\n   288\tlives there); \"user-positive\" is any id at or above it; \"tempid\" is any\n   289\tnegative integer.\n   290\t\n   291\t| Slot | `:resolved` | `:unresolved` |\n   292\t|---|---|---|\n   293\t| `e` | user-positive; reserved or tempid → defect | tempid → allocated; reserved or user-positive → defect (an unexplained positive reference: the writer cannot know the observer's ids) |\n   294\t| declared-ref `v` | user-positive or reserved; tempid → defect (this is the schema-aware check `local-datom?` alone does not make) | tempid → allocated; reserved passes; user-positive → defect |\n   295\t| `m` | reserved or user-positive; tempid → defect | reserved passes; tempid → allocated; user-positive → defect |\n   296\t| undeclared `v` | any value, untouched | any value, untouched |\n   297\t\n   298\tA composition that needs both kinds of medium in one query opens two index\n   299\tstates as two sources; `dao.space.query` keeps sources as separate\n   300\tdb-values and joins only where the query says so. **Equating `?e` across\n   301\tindependently allocated sources is never meaningful** — not across a\n   302\t`:resolved` and an `:unresolved` source, not across two `:unresolved`\n   303\tsessions with separate allocators, not across two `:resolved` media with\n   304\tdifferent writers — because unification compares integers and carries no\n   305\tallocation-domain check. This is advisory in the query (nothing can enforce\n   306\tit there) and therefore a contract on compositions: joins across sources go\n   307\tthrough values. Phase 2's shared allocator is a *composition-owned value\n   308\tthreaded serially* through the `:unresolved` sessions that share it — never\n   309\tan atom the sessions share — and only such sessions may equate `?e`.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   312\t\n   313\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n* —\n   314\twhichever slot it appeared in — it asserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   315\t\n   316\t```\n   317\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   318\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   319\t```\n   320\t\n   321\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   322\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   323\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   324\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. That is a **contract on\n   325\tmedia, not a check in the index**: a medium must not assert attributes in\n   326\t`:dao.space.index/*`. The index does not inspect attribute namespaces\n   327\t(decision 6), so a medium that violates the reservation shares `[e a v]`\n   328\tkeys with resolution facts and owns the resulting `current` semantics\n   329\t(greatest-`t` shadowing, or the `m`-conflict throw on a same-`t` meet).\n   330\tWith the reservation honoured, resolution facts and observed rows have\n   331\tdisjoint `[e a v]` keys and can neither shadow nor conflict. These are\n   332\tfacts *about* observation, distinct from the observed rows. They make\n   333\t\"which batch asserted this entity\" — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]` — a\n   334\tquery, and they are the raw material for cross-medium provenance.\n   335\t\n   336\t**Refs are batch-local; cross-medium coordinates are values.** Refs are\n   337\tresolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   338\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   339\ttoday — and a declared ref *always* resolves within the batch it appears\n   340\tin. There is no way, at attribute granularity, to say \"this `-16` names an\n   341\tentity on another medium\": the index would allocate a batch-local entity\n   342\tfor it, and a shared allocator only prevents *collisions*, it establishes\n   343\tno *correspondence*. So a coordinate into another medium must be carried as\n   344\ta **value**, qualified by the batch it refers to, under an attribute the\n   345\tschema does not declare a ref; the join is then value-based over\n   346\tresolution facts — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   347\t:dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — and is the composition's, since only the\n   348\tcomposition knows which observer's ordinal `t'` corresponds to the\n   349\tproducer's batch number. That correspondence is a constant offset **only\n   350\twhile neither session has seen a `gap`**; a gap count records that gaps\n   351\toccurred, not how many batches were lost, so after one the offset must be\n   352\tre-derived or provenance across it marked unknown (open question 2).\n   353\t\n   354\tThis has a consequence for `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1, recorded here as a\n   355\t**required amendment** to that document: its `:yin/source-call` is written\n   356\tas both an external coordinate (initial expansion: an eid in the source\n   357\tbatch) and a log-local ref (nested expansion: a node in the log copy). One\n   358\tattribute cannot be both. It must split — an undeclared value attribute for\n   359\tthe external coordinate, qualified by `:yin/source-batch` as it already is,\n   360\tand a declared-ref attribute for the log-local node — so that\n   361\t`event-schema` declares only the latter a ref and the index resolves it\n   362\tbatch-locally while leaving the former as the value the join needs. The\n   363\tschema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   364\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, the amended `event-schema`); the\n   365\tindex learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An\n   366\tundeclared attribute holding a negative number is a value, not a ref, and is\n   367\tleft alone.\n   368\t\n   369\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   370\t\n   371\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   372\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   373\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   374\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   375\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   376\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   377\t\n   378\t---\n   379\t\n   380\t## 4. Publication and the checkpoint\n   381\t\n   382\t### 4.1 Incremental publication\n   383\t\n   384\tBecause `:indexes` are persistent `dao.data.btree` values, publishing after\n   385\tbatch *n* is `bt/store-tree` over each tree plus one manifest — the same\n   386\tnode-blob format and intake path `publish-index!` uses today. What makes it\n   387\t*incremental* is that the trees keep their `:storage` across publishes:\n   388\t`store-tree` stores only the **dirty subgraph** (its docstring: \"no-op\n   389\treturning the existing address when the set is already stored\"), so nodes\n   390\tunchanged since the last publication are never re-stored and never\n   391\tre-appended. `publish-index!` today rebuilds into a *fresh* recording\n   392\thandle each time, which is why every node is dirty and every publish is\n   393\tO(tree). Two things persist across publishes in the index state, and they\n   394\tmust not be confused: the **stored-address marks on the trees' nodes**,\n   395\twhich are what `store-tree` consults (dirtiness lives in the tree, not in\n   396\tthe storage), and the **identity of `:storage`**, so that a stored node is\n   397\tnever re-stored under a second handle. What does *not* persist is the\n   398\trecording handle's content: the blobs it recorded since the last flush are\n   399\texactly the payloads to append, in store order, and `flush-staged` drains\n   400\tthem once the intake has accepted the manifest, so the handle never grows\n   401\twith history. Append cost is therefore proportional to what changed;\n   402\t`jing/materialize!`'s dedup at the far end is a second line of defence, not\n   403\tthe mechanism.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Draining is safe only if nothing ever refaults through the drained\n   406\thandle.** `store-tree` needs nothing from it (marks live in the tree), but\n   407\t`-store-tree!` also overwrites the set wrapper's *restore source* with the\n   408\tstorage it was given (`btree.cljc`, `set! storage storage'`), and\n   409\t`resident-root` refaults an evicted root through that field. On the JVM a\n   410\troot restored from a durable store is held by a soft reference (the\n   411\t`kv-storage` default ref-type there); on a resumed checkpoint session the\n   412\tsequence *resume → publish through the recording handle → flush → drain →\n   413\tGC clears the root → next fold or query refaults the root through the\n   414\tdrained handle* ends in \"missing index segment\". Child slots are unaffected\n   415\t(they fault through the durable store the settings name). The invariant the\n   416\tindex state must keep: **a tree's refaults resolve against durable content,\n   417\tnever against the recording handle.** The mechanism is that every tree an\n   418\tindex session holds carries `Settings` with ref-type `:strong`, so a root is\n   419\tnever evicted and never refaults — and *where those settings come from\n   420\tdiffers by session kind*, which is the whole subtlety. Ref-pinning consults\n   421\tthe **tree's** settings, not the storage passed to `store-tree`, so a\n   422\t`:strong` recording handle pins nothing by itself:\n   423\t\n   424\t- a session started at `:oldest` builds its trees either in memory (settings\n   425\t  carry no storage; `make-store-ref` pins the raw node) or through its\n   426\t  recording `kv-storage` constructed with `:ref-type :strong` — safe either\n   427\t  way;\n   428\t- a **resumed** session must restore its trees through a\n   429\t  session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the durable content store\n   430\t  (`bt/restore-tree` directly, behind an index-side helper) — **not**\n   431\t  through `query/open-published!` / `restored-indexes`, whose storage takes\n   432\t  the host default, `:soft` on the JVM, because those are the *read* path\n   433\t  and a query consumer wants eviction. Restoring through the read path would\n   434\t  reproduce the sequence above exactly.\n   435\t\n   436\tRe-restoring the trees from the durable store after each flush was\n   437\tconsidered and rejected — \"append success means enqueued, not materialized\"\n   438\t(`dao.space.index.md`), so the durable store may not yet hold the blobs the\n   439\tnew manifest names, and `-restore` throws on absence. cljs and cljd default\n   440\tto `:strong` already and never see the hazard.\n   441\t\n   442\t**The staged publication is a state machine, not a list.** `publish!`\n   443\tstages `{:payloads [blob₁ … blobₖ manifest] :next 0}` — the blobs recorded\n   444\tsince the last flush in store order, the manifest last — and `flush-staged`\n   445\tappends from `:next`, advancing it after each `ok`, so a payload the intake\n   446\thas accepted is never appended again by an ordinary retry. `full` on payload\n   447\t*i* leaves `:next = i` and the state not-ready; the next round resumes at\n   448\t*i*. `closed`, `invalid-value`, a malformed outcome, and `transport-error`\n   449\tare outcomes this forwarder cannot continue from: it throws, with the\n   450\tstaged state — including `:next` — preserved in the thrown value, so the\n   451\tcomposition decides and a retry after a repaired intake resumes rather than\n   452\trepeats. Manifest-last still holds, so a partial prefix is never a\n   453\tpublication. The guarantee is **at-most-once acceptance per staged\n   454\toccurrence under ordinary retries**; content-address deduplication at\n   455\t`jing/materialize!` is a separate guarantee that makes an *abnormal*\n   456\tre-append (after a lost session, §7) harmless, not exactly-once. The\n   457\trecording handle's content is drained only when the manifest has been\n   458\taccepted, i.e. when `:next` reaches the end.\n   459\t\n   460\t### 4.2 The checkpoint\n   461\t\n   462\t`dao.space.transactor.md` *Open items* asks that \"the log carries its own\n   463\tcheckpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume\". A\n   464\tcheckpoint here is a *value with a validity contract*, in three parts.\n   465\t\n   466\t**Capture at one boundary.** `publish!` is called between rounds, when the\n   467\tsession's cursor sits exactly after the last batch its trees cover; it\n   468\treturns, with the staged publication, a **candidate**\n   469\t\n   470\t```\n   471\t{:manifest-address a  :cursor c  :ids i  :batch n  :max-t t\n   472\t :ingress-gaps g  :defects d  :mode m  :schema-hash h}\n   473\t```\n   474\t\n   475\tcaptured together in that moment: the manifest that will name the trees,\n   476\tthe cursor after the last folded batch, the allocator, the ordinal, the\n   477\tgreatest writer `t` folded (§2.2 step 3), the session's gap and defect\n   478\tcounts, and the mode and schema the trees were built under. Nothing may be\n   479\tfolded between the publish and the capture, and nothing is: capture *is*\n   480\tthe publish's return value. A composition that recorded a cursor later than\n   481\tthe manifest's coverage would, on restart, skip every batch in between\n   482\tpermanently.\n   483\t\n   484\t**A candidate becomes a checkpoint only when it is recoverable.** Intake\n   485\tsuccess means enqueued, not materialized (`dao.space.index.md`); a process\n   486\tcan fail with the manifest, or blobs it names, still in an in-memory intake\n   487\t— exactly the loss window `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance documents. A\n   488\tcandidate is promoted to a checkpoint by the composition, and only after it\n   489\thas verified against the durable content store that the manifest is present\n   490\tand every address reachable from it (`bt/walk-addresses`) resolves. Until\n   491\tthen the previous checkpoint stands. Recovery from a latest publication that\n   492\tnever became recoverable is therefore never a special case: the composition\n   493\tresumes from the last *verified* checkpoint and re-folds the suffix.\n   494\t\n   495\t**Resumption is bound.** `restore` refuses a checkpoint whose `:mode` or\n   496\t`:schema-hash` differ from the session being constructed, restores the four\n   497\ttrees lazily through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the\n   498\tdurable store (§4.1 — not the query read path), reinstates `:ids`,\n   499\t`:batch`, `:max-t`, `:ingress-gaps` and `:defects` (a partial index stays\n   500\tpartial after restart; a fresh zero would make §3.2's offset check look safe\n   501\tagain), re-attaches at `c`, and continues folding. Nothing is replayed from\n   502\tthe origin.\n   503\t\n   504\t**The watermark.** The transactor's `create!` needs \"one plus the greatest\n   505\t`t` in the retained history\". A checkpoint covering `t ≤ 10` cannot answer\n   506\tthat alone if transactions through `t = 12` exist after its cursor. So the\n   507\tanswer is `:max-t` — a writer-time summary derived from the folded rows at\n   508\tcapture, causally bound to `:cursor`, never derived from observer ordinals\n   509\t— *plus the fold of the retained suffix after `c`* before writes are\n   510\tenabled. Three cases, distinct:\n   511\t\n   512\t- **Same logical stream, reopened in-process** (the writer-task restart\n   513\t  `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance names): restore the checkpoint, fold the\n   514\t  suffix from `c` to `blocked`, then `max-t + 1` is the watermark. This is\n   515\t  the O(suffix) relief the transactor asked for.\n   516\t- **Process restart over a durable medium** (`dao.stream.file.md`, cursors\n   517\t  survive): the same, over the reopened medium.\n   518\t- **Process restart over a memory-log**: a new, empty logical stream with a\n   519\t  new identity; the checkpoint's cursor names nothing on it, the session\n   520\t  starts at `:oldest`, and the watermark is the transactor's today (`0` for\n   521\t  empty history).\n   522\t\n   523\t\"One truth\" is honoured rather than assumed: `:max-t` is not a second\n   524\tcounter that can disagree with the log; it is the log's own `t`, summarized\n   525\tat a named cursor and only ever advanced by folding the log. Where the\n   526\tcheckpoint *record* lives — on the observed medium or beside the manifest\n   527\tin `dao.jing` — remains open (§8); its validity conditions above do not.\n   528\t\n   529\t---\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:6:   are explicit functions: materialize! computes the content address solely\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:8:   after the backend reports durability. get reads only :segment/sha256-...\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:12:   an explicit intake pool of dao.stream reader handles and materializes\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:251:(defn materialize!\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:258:     :inserted — the value is durably stored now;\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:375:   recovery cursor a gap report carries; nothing here resynchronizes\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:383:   payload, through dao.stream.observe/step with materialize! as the\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:388:       a payload was materialized; the member advanced to the successor\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:402:   member's next turn; nothing is auto-resynchronized. A defect carries the\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:410:   The effect is materialize!, which answers ok or throws: :failed is\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:420:                      :address (materialize! handle payload)})]\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:6:encoding, durable observer checkpoints, explicit materialization\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:8:and async hydration — is listed under *Open items and current limitations*.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:28:materializes their elements into content-addressed key-value storage.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:60:hash, and materialize in a key/value store. That operation assigns content\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:93:written into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:136:intake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous observer has yet\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:137:materialized those payloads; see *Open items and current limitations*.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:162:The implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:166:nothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:261:| `:dao.stream/ok`                                          | a payload was materialized                                 | `:address`                                      | successor, from the step |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:295:are explicit functions, not a protocol or hidden state. `dao.jing/materialize!`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:323:  `{op fn}` map — at a WebSocket endpoint. The synchronous client is a\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:402:  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:404:- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:405:  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:406:  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:407:  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:412:- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:414:  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:415:  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:420:  `request-materialize` / `step` / `abandon` shape — a client the caller\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:421:  steps on a host that cannot wait. It is owed to the async hydration\n  2101\t(defn walk-addresses\n  2102\t  \"Call on-address with every segment key reachable from address, the\n  2103\t   root's own address first; descend into a subtree only while on-address\n  2104\t   returns truthy (psset's prune protocol — lets a GC mark phase skip\n  2105\t   subtrees shared with already-marked versions). nil address => nothing.\"\n  2106\t  [storage address on-address]\n  2107\t  (letfn [(walk-node\n  2108\t            [n]\n  2109\t            (when (branch? n)\n  2110\t              (let [as (node-addresses-arr n)\n  2111\t                    l (node-len n)\n  2112\t                    deep? (> (node-level n) 1)]\n  2113\t                (dotimes [i l]\n  2114\t                  (let [a (arr/aget as i)]\n  2115\t                    (when (and (on-address a) deep?)\n  2116\t                      (walk-node (-restore storage a))))))))]\n  2117\t    (when (some? address)\n  2118\t      (when (on-address address) (walk-node (-restore storage address))))))\n  2119\t\n  2120\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc | sed -n '52,89p;117,132p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '250,293p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '156,188p;389,416p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '183,208p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc | sed -n '14,57p'; git diff --check HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc | sed -n '52,89p;117,132p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '250,293p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '156,188p;389,416p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '183,208p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc | sed -n '14,57p'; git diff --check HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md\"","aggregated_output":"    52\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    53\t            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\t(defn- entity->datoms\n    57\t  \"One datom per k/v pair, all sharing transaction time `t`. `:db/id` is\n    58\t   required: a durable log cannot mint per-batch tempids without colliding\n    59\t   across appends. Attribute order within one entity's own datoms is\n    60\t   whatever the host's map iteration yields — unspecified across platforms.\"\n    61\t  [m t]\n    62\t  (when-not (contains? m :db/id)\n    63\t    (throw (ex-info \"entity map requires :db/id\" {:entity m})))\n    64\t  (let [e (:db/id m)]\n    65\t    (mapv (fn [[a v]]\n    66\t            (let [d [e a v t datom/default-op]]\n    67\t              (when-not (datom/local-datom? d)\n    68\t                (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n    69\t                                {:datom d, :entity m})))\n    70\t              d))\n    71\t          (remove (fn [[a _v]] (= a :db/id)) m))))\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn- pad-datom\n    75\t  \"Pad [e a v] / [e a v t m] to the canonical 5-tuple. The transactor owns\n    76\t   transaction time: a non-nil explicit t is rejected, a nil t is stamped\n    77\t   with the allocated `t`. A nil m takes the default assertion op; an\n    78\t   explicit m (0, a retraction) is preserved.\"\n    79\t  [d t]\n    80\t  (let [[e a v dt dm] d]\n    81\t    (when-not (nil? dt)\n    82\t      (throw\n    83\t        (ex-info\n    84\t          \"the transactor owns transaction time: explicit datom t is rejected\"\n    85\t          {:datom d})))\n    86\t    (let [padded [e a v t (if (nil? dm) datom/default-op dm)]]\n    87\t      (when-not (datom/local-datom? padded)\n    88\t        (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n    89\t                        {:datom padded, :input d})))\n   117\t(defn- derive-next-t\n   118\t  \"Derive the next transaction time from a flattened retained history: 0 for\n   119\t   an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t. A datom t that is not a\n   120\t   non-negative integer is a malformed history and throws — this scan is the\n   121\t   causality boundary of the value.\"\n   122\t  [datoms]\n   123\t  (if (empty? datoms)\n   124\t    0\n   125\t    (let [ts (mapv index/datom-t datoms)]\n   126\t      (when-not (every? #(and (integer? %) (<= 0 %)) ts)\n   127\t        (throw\n   128\t          (ex-info\n   129\t            \"malformed retained history: datom t must be a non-negative integer\"\n   130\t            {:history datoms})))\n   131\t      (inc (reduce max ts)))))\n   132\t\n   250\t\n   251\t(defn materialize!\n   252\t  \"Content-address payload and store it through the handle's backend.\n   253\t\n   254\t   The address is derived solely from the payload via segment-key, and the\n   255\t   backend effect :put-content-fn is invoked as (put-content-fn address\n   256\t   payload). The backend must answer with one of:\n   257\t\n   258\t     :inserted — the value is durably stored now;\n   259\t     :present  — an equal value is already stored under that address.\n   260\t\n   261\t   Any other answer is an invalid backend result and throws: DaoJing does\n   262\t   not accept ambiguous truthiness. The address is returned only after the\n   263\t   backend reports success.\n   264\t\n   265\t   On :present the stored value is read back through :get-content-fn and\n   266\t   verified. Equal content is idempotent and returns the same address.\n   267\t   Unequal content at the same address is an integrity failure and throws\n   268\t   loudly. Nothing is ever overwritten.\"\n   269\t  [handle payload]\n   270\t  (let [put (:put-content-fn handle)\n   271\t        get-fn (:get-content-fn handle)]\n   272\t    (when-not (fn? put)\n   273\t      (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing handle requires :put-content-fn\"\n   274\t                      {:handle handle})))\n   275\t    (when-not (fn? get-fn)\n   276\t      (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing handle requires :get-content-fn\"\n   277\t                      {:handle handle})))\n   278\t    (let [address (segment-key payload)\n   279\t          result (put address payload)]\n   280\t      (case result\n   281\t        :inserted address\n   282\t        :present\n   283\t        (let [stored (get-fn address content-missing)]\n   284\t          (cond\n   285\t            (identical? stored content-missing)\n   286\t            (throw\n   287\t              (ex-info\n   288\t                \"backend reported :present but the content address is absent\"\n   289\t                {:address address, :payload payload}))\n   290\t            (= stored payload) address\n   291\t            :else\n   292\t            (throw\n   293\t              (ex-info\n   156\t- pool streams may be consumed in any interleaving;\n   157\t- duplicate publication is harmless;\n   158\t- immutable content writes do not require cross-stream coordination; and\n   159\t- a materialization can be reconstructed by replaying the available pool\n   160\t  streams.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n   163\tthe payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n   164\tfor an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n   165\tbe a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n   166\tnothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n   167\t`:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n   168\tstored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n   169\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   170\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   171\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   172\t\n   173\t### Canonical encoding\n   174\t\n   175\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   176\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   177\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   178\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   179\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   180\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   181\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   182\t\n   183\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   184\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   185\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   188\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   389\t\n   390\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized `pr-str` encoder must be\n   391\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   392\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   393\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   394\t  and every minted address change together.\n   395\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   396\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   397\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n   398\t  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n   399\t  the contract). A runner that drives the loop over time remains to be built.\n   400\t- **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   401\t  that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   402\t  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   403\t  explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n   404\t- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\n   405\t  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\n   406\t  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\n   407\t  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\n   408\t  write-path redesign.\n   409\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   410\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   411\t  forever.\n   412\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   413\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   414\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   415\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   416\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   183\t  (loop [{:keys [observer consumer]} session]\n   184\t    (if-not (ready? consumer)\n   185\t      (let [consumer' (run consumer)]\n   186\t        (if (ready? consumer')\n   187\t          (recur {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'})\n   188\t          {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'}))\n   189\t      ;; The load is the step's effect, so the cursor advances only once it has\n   190\t      ;; returned: a throwing load propagates before any successor exists.\n   191\t      (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   192\t                                   (:cursor observer)\n   193\t                                   (fn [batch]\n   194\t                                     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\n   195\t                                      :dao.stream.observer/loaded\n   196\t                                      (load consumer batch)}))]\n   197\t        (case (:status observed)\n   198\t          :advance (let [consumer' (run (:dao.stream.observer/loaded\n   199\t                                          (:effect observed)))\n   200\t                         observer' (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))]\n   201\t                     (if (ready? consumer')\n   202\t                       (recur {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'})\n   203\t                       {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'}))\n   204\t          :gap (recur {:observer (adopt-gap observer observed),\n   205\t                       :consumer consumer})\n   206\t          ;; :retry (blocked) and :ended retain the cursor and end the round.\n   207\t          (:retry :ended) {:observer observer, :consumer consumer}\n   208\t          (observation-terminal observed observer))))))\n    14\t(def reserved\n    15\t  \"Reserved metadata-entity ids (ident -> id) for the m slot.\n    16\t   0/1 mirror Datomic's `added` boolean; 2 extends it.\"\n    17\t  {:db/retract 0, ; added=false\n    18\t   :db/assert 1,  ; added=true (emit default)\n    19\t   :db/derived 2})  ; computed/derived; excluded from content hash\n    20\t\n    21\t\n    22\t(def default-op\n    23\t  \"The m written for emitted datoms when no metadata is supplied: assertion.\"\n    24\t  (:db/assert reserved))\n    25\t\n    26\t\n    27\t(def first-user-id\n    28\t  \"Lowest entity id available to user entities; 0 through 15 are reserved.\n    29\t\n    30\t   The block is 16 wide rather than 1024 because only the m-slot markers need\n    31\t   globally fixed numbers. Built-in attributes and type markers live in the a\n    32\t   and v slots as namespaced keywords, already globally meaningful without an\n    33\t   id, and a schema entity in e position takes an ordinary stream-local id\n    34\t   named across streams by its :db/ident. Reserving more would put global\n    35\t   coordinates in a slot the spec declares a stream-local gauge, and would put\n    36\t   the floor above what an int8-wide e can represent at all.\n    37\t\n    38\t   See docs/design/datom.md, Reserved Entities.\"\n    39\t  16)\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(defn local-datom?\n    43\t  \"True for the runtime-enforced portion of a persisted local d5 datom:\n    44\t   `[e a v t m]`, with non-negative integer stream-local coordinates `e` and\n    45\t   `t`, integer `m`, and a namespaced keyword attribute. Negative tempids are\n    46\t   compilation values and must be resolved before commitment. Canonical value\n    47\t   encoding is a separate persistence concern and is not decided here.\"\n    48\t  [x]\n    49\t  (and (vector? x)\n    50\t       (= 5 (count x))\n    51\t       (integer? (nth x 0))\n    52\t       (not (neg? (nth x 0)))\n    53\t       (keyword? (nth x 1))\n    54\t       (some? (namespace (nth x 1)))\n    55\t       (integer? (nth x 3))\n    56\t       (not (neg? (nth x 3)))\n    57\t       (integer? (nth x 4))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Most of A1–A11 are addressed. Checkpoint promotion can be verified independently through durable-store reads, and rejecting positive user `m` references is consistent with unresolved mode. I found remaining gaps: checkpoint capture needs observer fields absent from its proposed input, draining defects erases the only rejection record, and the reserved-id prose contradicts the matrix. I’m also checking empty-batch and shared-allocator recovery cases."}}
